00:36:00.220 --> 00:36:07.180
i'll be a trainee computer programmer and are you not programming his computer oh yes i am

00:36:07.180 --> 00:36:13.150
programming his computer i'm also an apprentice pool bearer a fully fledged chief mourner and i

00:36:13.150 --> 00:36:18.880
have to go and get the sandwiches long as you've got job satisfaction that's the main thing yes well

00:36:18.880 --> 00:36:24.340
i have not got job satisfaction actually matter of fact i'm thinking of resigning well you better

00:36:24.340 --> 00:36:26.470
You better hurry up before he sacks you.

00:36:26.470 --> 00:36:28.810
Look, that big traffic jam was not my fault.

00:36:28.810 --> 00:36:30.000
All right, you name one person.

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:31.560
- The person who blamed me.

00:36:31.560 --> 00:36:32.900
- Mr Jahan did.

00:36:32.900 --> 00:36:34.360
- Grieving relatives did.

00:36:34.360 --> 00:36:35.900
- The flyin' I did.

00:36:39.240 --> 00:36:42.500
- I thought the differential on that Cortina seized up.

00:36:42.500 --> 00:36:45.080
Well, I called for the RAC and they called for a tow truck

00:36:45.080 --> 00:36:46.260
but it couldn't get through.

00:36:46.260 --> 00:36:47.180
- Why?

00:36:47.180 --> 00:36:50.100
- Well, 'cause of the big traffic jam.

00:36:50.100 --> 00:36:51.940
Anyway, it all went off all right in the end.

00:36:51.940 --> 00:36:53.700
- Oh, yeah, it went off all right in the end.

00:36:53.700 --> 00:36:55.640
I mean, you could have started a trend.

00:36:55.640 --> 00:36:59.540
Floodlit funerals could be all the rage.

00:37:00.990 --> 00:37:04.810
I don't want to talk no more about it. I've got more important things on my mind.

00:37:05.050 --> 00:37:08.610
Oh, we're not getting back to Freddy the Bleeding Frog again, are we?

00:37:08.790 --> 00:37:10.410
Yes, we are.

00:37:11.250 --> 00:37:14.290
There's a million quid's worth of gold bullion out there, and it's mine. It's ours.

00:37:15.370 --> 00:37:16.950
You've got something else you are.

00:37:17.160 --> 00:37:21.240
A drunken old woman spends you some cock and bull story, and you fall for it.

00:37:22.090 --> 00:37:26.730
Now, listen, Rodney, just because Renie was a good time girl who liked the occasional ties her,

00:37:26.910 --> 00:37:28.330
that was all right. She was never a liar.

00:37:29.000 --> 00:37:30.000
Anyway, I've been...

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:34.750
out of seeing a few faces this afternoon. I've got some information. They have confirmed

00:37:34.750 --> 00:37:41.430
it, right, about a robbery, the gold bullion never being found, the lot. How can you be

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:46.400
sure it ain't been found? I mean, over the past few years, every policeman and underworld

00:37:46.400 --> 00:37:50.110
figure in the country must have been looking for that gold. Yeah, and what would they have

00:37:50.110 --> 00:37:54.780
done with it, eh? They'd either put it through a fence, which meant it becomes public knowledge,

00:37:54.780 --> 00:37:59.700
right, or they smelt it down themselves, right, and if they do that, well, they're

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:04.390
amount of gold coming onto the market Rodney calls his ripples, right? The sort of ripples

00:38:04.390 --> 00:38:10.580
that would be remembered for a long time. What if the police found it? I'm talking about the

00:38:10.580 --> 00:38:20.810
police. The chaps never found it either. I had a chat with the Driscoll brothers. You went and

00:38:20.810 --> 00:38:30.000
saw the Driscoll brothers? Why, what are they like? God, what is smashing bloke sunk? It's like bumping into the two Ronnies, Biggs and Cray.

00:38:30.350 --> 00:38:33.050
I never sussed out why you was asking questions.

00:38:33.050 --> 00:38:34.050
No.

00:38:34.050 --> 00:38:38.820
I mean, you know what they're like, don't you?

00:38:38.820 --> 00:38:44.300
A couple of years ago, right, some guru and the world would end within a month.

00:38:44.300 --> 00:38:50.780
And Danny Driscoll better grand that it would.

00:38:50.780 --> 00:38:52.780
And he's the brains of the earth.

00:38:52.780 --> 00:38:58.820
No, the only trouble was, I kept on having to refer to him as Freddy the Frog.

00:38:58.820 --> 00:39:00.000
I couldn't remember his real son.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:02.930
It was, I don't know, Robson or something, wasn't it?

00:39:02.930 --> 00:39:03.820
Robdall.

00:39:03.820 --> 00:39:04.820
Robdall.

00:39:04.820 --> 00:39:06.290
That's what it was, yeah.

00:39:06.290 --> 00:39:08.740
I've been trying to think of that all afternoon.

00:39:08.740 --> 00:39:17.640
Wait a minute, did you know him?

00:39:17.640 --> 00:39:18.720
Yeah, vaguely.

00:39:18.720 --> 00:39:22.560
Well, why didn't you say?

00:39:22.560 --> 00:39:29.090
Well, first time I met him, he was just a kid, 18, 19, different age group from me, then

00:39:29.090 --> 00:39:30.000
over the years, I used to say.

00:39:30.030 --> 00:39:35.230
to bump into him every so often, usually in one of the pubs down the uh, down the docks.

00:39:35.230 --> 00:39:43.430
Ah, he was a very likeable bloke, very generous, he was uh, very handsome, everybody liked

00:39:43.430 --> 00:39:46.870
him, especially the women, they used to fall over themselves for him.

00:39:46.870 --> 00:39:49.970
Yes, yes, yes, yes, what about the gold bullion?

00:39:49.970 --> 00:39:52.080
Well, he robbed a bank in the city.

00:39:52.080 --> 00:39:55.060
Yeah, we know all that, don't we?

00:39:55.060 --> 00:39:57.260
What happened after the robbery?

00:39:57.260 --> 00:40:00.000
Well, about a week after,

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:04.000
Freddy and an explosives expert called Jelly Kelly,

00:40:04.000 --> 00:40:08.870
they broke into a post office in Plumstead.

00:40:08.870 --> 00:40:09.900
Yeah, that's right.

00:40:09.900 --> 00:40:13.860
And then apparently they set the wires of the explosives all right,

00:40:13.860 --> 00:40:18.160
everything going well, when, nobody knew why,

00:40:18.160 --> 00:40:23.090
Freddy the Frog sat on a detonator.

00:40:23.090 --> 00:40:30.000
They found him up on a roof of a building across the road.

00:40:30.600 --> 00:40:34.120
all right what happened to the other mush kelly the jelly

00:40:34.120 --> 00:40:39.880
well he was holding the nitroglycerine when freddy sat down

00:40:39.880 --> 00:40:53.170
so he didn't survive either well if he did he'd be no good in a mexican way

00:40:54.300 --> 00:40:59.860
wherever i go it's the same story ain't it freddy the frog took the secret with him

00:41:00.030 --> 00:41:01.670
Maybe he shipped the gold abroad.

00:41:01.810 --> 00:41:03.310
No, there wouldn't have been time.

00:41:03.670 --> 00:41:06.850
There was only a week between him doing the job and hitting the snooze button, wasn't there?

00:41:09.030 --> 00:41:11.720
Do you know, there's another thing that's confusing me and all.

00:41:12.560 --> 00:41:16.120
I mean, if that Freddy the Frog was going out with a married woman on this estate,

00:41:16.700 --> 00:41:18.900
why did he leave all his money to our mum?

00:41:23.490 --> 00:41:24.370
Mystery, isn't it?

00:41:25.960 --> 00:41:27.980
I'll make some tea.

00:41:29.620 --> 00:41:30.000
Innocent.

00:41:30.060 --> 00:41:35.880
Another thing, I was talking to one of them Driscoll Heavies, and he reckoned that Freddy, he had a son by that woman.

00:41:37.270 --> 00:41:41.840
It's just a rumour, Del. I take no notice of rumours.

00:41:41.940 --> 00:41:46.780
No, no, no. But I mean, if it was true, that boy would be in his mid-twenties by now, wouldn't he?

00:41:49.980 --> 00:41:55.100
Still, the thing is, he don't know who his dad was, so he can't make a claim on the fortune, can he?

00:41:57.630 --> 00:41:59.840
Oh, look. Look at this, Rod. Oh, there you are.

00:42:00.410 --> 00:42:05.150
Look at this Rodney, look, here we are, millionaires, and we're getting fretting letters from the milkman.

00:42:07.210 --> 00:42:11.560
This Freddy the Frog, did he have any hobbies or pastimes?

00:42:11.560 --> 00:42:14.420
Did he have any hobbies or pastimes?

00:42:14.420 --> 00:42:17.580
We're looking for his gold, not his rugby tennis racket!

00:42:17.580 --> 00:42:22.380
No, no, I just thought, you know, the more you find out about him, the more you know how his mind works.

00:42:22.380 --> 00:42:25.760
Oh, all right, yes, good thinking Rodney.

00:42:25.760 --> 00:42:29.180
Right, well, he was a bit of an artist or something, wasn't he, Albert?

00:42:30.350 --> 00:42:32.070
Yeah, a good artist, yeah.

00:42:32.590 --> 00:42:36.250
They reckon that if he hadn't have been a tea leaf, he could have made a very good forger.

00:42:37.880 --> 00:42:39.990
Well, that's got us a lot closer, ain't it, eh?

00:42:40.670 --> 00:42:41.550
Problem solved it.

00:42:42.520 --> 00:42:43.660
I think I'm going to go to bed.

00:42:43.900 --> 00:42:45.060
Say goodnight to you two.

00:42:45.580 --> 00:42:45.860
Goodnight.

00:42:46.250 --> 00:42:46.570
Night.

00:42:51.100 --> 00:42:51.420
Albert.

00:42:52.140 --> 00:42:53.700
They're rumours, Rodney.

00:42:54.310 --> 00:42:54.890
That's all.

00:42:56.980 --> 00:42:57.420
Rumours.

00:42:59.030 --> 00:42:59.690
Goodnight, son.

00:43:07.040 --> 00:43:10.460
Don't forget, you've got to be down a morgue by half nine.

00:43:11.490 --> 00:43:13.550
All right, come on.

00:43:14.320 --> 00:43:17.620
We'll just finish this and we'll pop down the market and do a bit with the massagers.

00:43:18.020 --> 00:43:21.900
And this time, when I've finished operating on you, no tap dancing.

00:43:22.350 --> 00:43:24.290
Just straighten up slowly like it's a miracle.

00:43:25.430 --> 00:43:26.900
Now, got it!

00:43:27.050 --> 00:43:27.790
What's all that about?

00:43:28.030 --> 00:43:30.000
I don't know. Looks like peace in our time.

00:43:33.070 --> 00:43:37.690
This morning, Mr. Jahan asked me to transfer all his old paper files onto a computer.

00:43:37.690 --> 00:43:38.690
Not my computer?

00:43:38.690 --> 00:43:39.610
Yeah.

00:43:39.610 --> 00:43:40.610
Now, have a read of that.

00:43:40.610 --> 00:43:41.610
No, no, it's a summons.

00:43:41.610 --> 00:43:42.610
It's a summons, isn't it?

00:43:42.610 --> 00:43:43.610
No, no, no.

00:43:43.610 --> 00:43:49.520
It's one of Mr. Jahan's order forms from July 1963.

00:43:49.520 --> 00:43:53.120
Now look who ordered one coffin to be specially made.

00:43:53.120 --> 00:44:00.000
One Frederick Robdall, but it was ordered five weeks before he did the robbery, six weeks

00:44:00.000 --> 00:44:01.380
before he blew himself up.

00:44:01.380 --> 00:44:03.150
Well, don't you see what it means?

00:44:03.150 --> 00:44:04.550
Yeah, he had a premonition.

00:44:06.900 --> 00:44:14.390
No, no, wait a minute, wait a minute, look, look.

00:44:14.390 --> 00:44:18.190
This casket here was ordered by Mr. Frederick Robdell, right?

00:44:18.190 --> 00:44:22.230
He paid for it, but it was made for a Mr. Alfred Broderick.

00:44:22.230 --> 00:44:24.850
- Who's Alfred Broderick? - No.

00:44:24.850 --> 00:44:27.170
Look at the two names closely.

00:44:27.170 --> 00:44:29.440
It's an anagram.

00:44:31.150 --> 00:44:32.510
Yeah, yeah, I can see that.

00:44:33.970 --> 00:44:35.220
But who was he?

00:44:37.310 --> 00:44:39.220
No, he's...

00:44:39.220 --> 00:44:40.320
Oh, bloody hell, look.

00:44:41.480 --> 00:44:43.920
If you transpose all the letters...

00:44:43.920 --> 00:44:44.360
Transp...

00:44:44.360 --> 00:44:45.100
Mix up.

00:44:45.120 --> 00:44:45.500
Mix them up, yeah, yeah.

00:44:45.500 --> 00:44:47.920
You mix up all the letters from Frederick Rodden, right?

00:44:48.080 --> 00:44:50.420
It becomes Alfred Broderick.

00:44:50.850 --> 00:44:52.930
In other words, Broderick never existed.

00:44:53.050 --> 00:44:54.410
He's just one of Freddy's aliases.

00:44:55.070 --> 00:44:56.210
He's right and all.

00:44:58.190 --> 00:45:00.000
So what you're saying is,

00:45:01.310 --> 00:45:05.170
He put the bullion into the coffin

00:45:05.170 --> 00:45:07.510
and then got the co-op to hide it for him.

00:45:07.510 --> 00:45:09.110
Or in this case, Mr Jahan.

00:45:09.110 --> 00:45:09.950
- That's right.

00:45:09.950 --> 00:45:11.510
It was all legal above board.

00:45:11.510 --> 00:45:13.770
It was most probably paraded through them streets.

00:45:13.770 --> 00:45:15.070
- I bet he even got the old bill

00:45:15.070 --> 00:45:16.610
to hold up the traffic for it.

00:45:17.750 --> 00:45:20.630
- And then it was buried with all the usual honors.

00:45:20.630 --> 00:45:22.450
All Freddie had to do was bide his time

00:45:22.450 --> 00:45:23.990
and then come back for it.

00:45:23.990 --> 00:45:26.070
- You two seem to be forgetting something.

00:45:26.070 --> 00:45:28.720
This isn't the family pet you're talking about.

00:45:28.720 --> 00:45:30.000
If what you're suggesting

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:34.880
right. He'd have needed permission from the authorities. He'd have needed official documentation

00:45:34.880 --> 00:45:43.190
and lots and lots of it. So where's he get all that? He always has to spoil things, don't

00:45:43.190 --> 00:45:52.420
he? Wait a minute, wait a minute. I think I've cracked it. Listen, way back in the early

00:45:52.420 --> 00:45:58.910
60s when you was just a nipper, mum got herself a job down a town hall as a secretary. A secretary?

00:45:59.340 --> 00:46:00.000
Yeah, yes.

00:46:00.480 --> 00:46:04.640
one of her duties was hoovering out a registrar's department, right?

00:46:05.770 --> 00:46:09.420
See what he'd mean? So she could get her hands on all the documents that he needed

00:46:09.420 --> 00:46:11.720
and mark them with the official stamp.

00:46:12.000 --> 00:46:12.680
Oh, dear.

00:46:12.900 --> 00:46:13.360
Of course.

00:46:13.380 --> 00:46:13.780
Rodney.

00:46:14.320 --> 00:46:15.560
What are you doing here?

00:46:16.060 --> 00:46:18.370
I thought you were supposed to be helping me with the embalming.

00:46:18.530 --> 00:46:20.810
Yeah, sorry, Mr. Jan. I took an early lunch.

00:46:21.010 --> 00:46:21.870
It's all right, Mr. Jehan. No.

00:46:22.010 --> 00:46:23.930
Gee, please, just come and sit down for a minute, will you?

00:46:24.230 --> 00:46:26.730
Because I'd just like to have a little chat with you.

00:46:26.730 --> 00:46:29.510
Look, Rodney found this in your files.

00:46:30.830 --> 00:46:34.730
this is confidential material. I know, it just shows you how keen he is though. He's

00:46:34.730 --> 00:46:38.250
bringing his homework to lunch with him. Listen, do you remember, do you remember

00:46:38.250 --> 00:46:44.210
this man, Mr. Frederick Robdell? Yes, I remember him very well. Most charming

00:46:44.210 --> 00:46:48.920
man. See, my father had just bought the business and Mr. Robdell was one of our

00:46:48.920 --> 00:46:53.960
first clients. The other reason it sticks out in my mind is because Mr. Robdell

00:46:53.960 --> 00:47:00.000
ordered an extra large casket to be made. Ah, and I

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:04.570
I expect his mate, Mr. Broderick, was an extra large chap.

00:47:04.570 --> 00:47:05.710
Was he?

00:47:05.710 --> 00:47:06.710
I wouldn't know.

00:47:06.710 --> 00:47:07.710
We didn't handle the funeral.

00:47:07.710 --> 00:47:09.480
What?

00:47:09.480 --> 00:47:13.440
We simply supplied the casket.

00:47:13.440 --> 00:47:16.050
Mr. Robdor came and collected in a van one night.

00:47:16.050 --> 00:47:19.630
You mean it was a take away?

00:47:19.630 --> 00:47:22.670
Mr. Robdor told us it was to be a very private affair.

00:47:22.670 --> 00:47:27.040
We didn't question his decision, we didn't want to intrude upon his grief, and we needed

00:47:27.040 --> 00:47:28.540
the business.

00:47:28.540 --> 00:47:29.540
Wait a minute, wait a minute.

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:32.840
Does that mean that you don't know where it's buried?

00:47:33.140 --> 00:47:35.520
As I said, I don't know. It was a private affair.

00:47:35.980 --> 00:47:37.980
I must go now. Rodney, how long will you be?

00:47:38.220 --> 00:47:39.180
Back soon, Mr. James.

00:47:39.200 --> 00:47:40.060
We have a lot of work to be done.

00:47:40.080 --> 00:47:40.600
Yeah, I know.

00:47:44.090 --> 00:47:45.090
When I see the end of that, then.

00:47:45.520 --> 00:47:46.780
We never find it now, son.

00:47:46.920 --> 00:47:47.700
Yes, we will.

00:47:47.980 --> 00:47:51.430
Dale, the gold has been missing for 24 years.

00:47:51.630 --> 00:47:54.890
And the last thing anyone saw of Freddy the Frog was on a radar screen.

00:47:54.970 --> 00:47:56.010
So what chance have we got?

00:47:57.630 --> 00:48:00.000
Listen to me.

00:48:01.180 --> 00:48:02.600
He would have buried it somewhere local.

00:48:03.480 --> 00:48:05.620
He would have stuck to an area that he knew well.

00:48:06.570 --> 00:48:07.890
Listen, this is what we're going to do.

00:48:08.770 --> 00:48:10.810
I'm going to go down and see the flower man down the market

00:48:10.810 --> 00:48:13.210
and get us a codgel of tulips on sale or return.

00:48:13.970 --> 00:48:17.230
Then you're going to go and visit every graveyard and cemetery in the district

00:48:17.230 --> 00:48:19.750
and you're going to read every name on every headstone.

00:48:20.030 --> 00:48:22.410
And when you leave, you leave a flower as if you're one of the relatives.

00:48:23.010 --> 00:48:24.150
But it's fast, John.

00:48:24.490 --> 00:48:27.100
Well, all right, if anyone asks you, say you come from a big family.

00:48:28.730 --> 00:48:30.000
Well, Lee, I want you to do this.

00:48:30.000 --> 00:48:36.000
in your travels. A bit like a busman's holiday for you, isn't it? In the meantime, I'm going

00:48:36.000 --> 00:48:41.180
to check the records down at town halls, stonemasons, churches, that sort of thing. Don't worry,

00:48:41.180 --> 00:48:45.800
brother, don't worry. We'll find it, we will, we'll find it. Del, I don't want to be the

00:48:45.800 --> 00:48:50.900
prophet of doom or nothing, but I do get the feeling we are wasting our time. That is time

00:48:50.900 --> 00:48:56.090
that could be spent more productively in earning some money and paying some bills. We owe two

00:48:56.090 --> 00:49:00.000
months' rent, we are drinking tea with no milk in it, and the electricity ball keep

00:49:00.000 --> 00:49:02.120
calling round to see why their meter is running backwards.

00:49:03.530 --> 00:49:05.470
There's food in the cupboard, isn't there?

00:49:05.730 --> 00:49:08.410
Yeah, thanks to my petron and Rodney's wages.

00:49:08.860 --> 00:49:11.600
It wouldn't be a bad idea for you to drive down to Hampshire

00:49:11.600 --> 00:49:13.940
and pick up that computer money off the vicar.

00:49:14.710 --> 00:49:16.010
Oh, leave it out.

00:49:16.650 --> 00:49:18.780
That gold must be here, hidden somewhere.

00:49:19.120 --> 00:49:20.620
I mean, it can't have disappeared.

00:49:20.740 --> 00:49:22.960
This is Peckham, not the Bermuda Triangle.

00:49:23.910 --> 00:49:26.310
And as for me, going all the way down to Hampshire

00:49:26.310 --> 00:49:28.550
to pick up a peddling 120 quid,

00:49:28.670 --> 00:49:30.000
that's like admitting defeat.

00:49:30.480 --> 00:49:33.880
A sign that I'd given up all hope of ever finding my birthright.

00:49:35.460 --> 00:49:36.860
That is not my style.

00:49:39.080 --> 00:49:43.130
When Del Trotter says he's going to do something, Del Trotter does it.

00:49:46.870 --> 00:49:48.290
You see what I mean, Mr Trotter?

00:49:48.330 --> 00:49:50.810
I've tried everything and it simply refuses to work.

00:49:54.220 --> 00:49:55.780
Has it received a whack of any kind?

00:49:55.940 --> 00:49:57.020
Oh, no, I can assure you.

00:50:01.150 --> 00:50:02.540
That's all it needed.

00:50:02.540 --> 00:50:03.790
Yes.

00:50:03.790 --> 00:50:05.790
Of course, I'm not technically minded like you, Mr. Trotter.

00:50:05.790 --> 00:50:08.790
No, well, some of these high-tech advancements, they need a bit of encouragement.

00:50:08.790 --> 00:50:11.680
Well, if you'll just give me my money, I'll bid you a fond farewell.

00:50:11.680 --> 00:50:14.680
I'm afraid I shan't be needing the computer, Mr. Trotter.

00:50:14.680 --> 00:50:21.260
As I said to you at the wedding, I couldn't honestly see what part a computer could play

00:50:21.260 --> 00:50:23.260
in the daily running of a small parish such as this.

00:50:23.260 --> 00:50:25.270
And my words have been borne out.

